3b) the choice that actually moves the numbers: rank states by the goal count or by the score
 budget x state ranking  spearman   mae  satisfied  mean score  passages  CVs scored below the ranking
      0.5          goal     0.724 32.42       1.27        26.9      1.00                             7
      0.5      coverage     0.724 32.42       1.27        26.9      1.00                             7
      1.0          goal     0.752 28.67       1.70        30.7      1.12                             2
      1.0      coverage     0.777 28.39       1.61        30.9      1.12                             0
      2.0          goal     0.789 23.30       2.33        37.1      1.82                             0
      2.0      coverage     0.786 23.06       2.27        37.3      1.79                             0
      4.0          goal     0.787 22.45       2.36        37.9      1.91                             0
      4.0      coverage     0.787 22.45       2.36        37.9      1.91                             0
   more words can mean a lower score: across the 99 budget steps of this grid, the `goal` ranking leaves a CV below the score it earned one step earlier 3 times and `coverage` 0 times. A bigger budget is supposed to be a floor under the score, so the goal predicate costs something a person would notice - and it is a search artefact, not a law of the problem: the search keeps the best node it saw at any depth, so the only way a wider budget can lose is by changing which nodes the beam keeps.
   `goal` is the classical one - a requirement is a predicate, either satisfied or not - and it can prefer one clean hit over more spread-out partial credit. `coverage` ranks on the graded mean, which is what the score column is made from, and the last column counts where that difference shows up.

